---
import PageTitle from "@/components/PageTitle.astro";
import PageLayout from "@/layouts/Base.astro";
import "@/styles/explore.css";

const meta = {
	description:
		"Two dark-matter universes grown from the same starting conditions with different cosmologies. Run time forward from redshift 45 to today and watch them come apart.",
	title: "Watching a universe grow",
	// The fiducial run at three epochs, left to right: nearly smooth, filaments
	// forming, the web today. The other demos both reuse /images/PCA.webp, which
	// would say nothing about this page.
	ogImage: "/images/structure-growth.webp",
};
---

{/*
	A space between a text node and a following element is TRIMMED by Astro, so
	`holds\n<span>` renders as "holds2,097,152". Every `<span data-fill>` below
	therefore begins its line with the preceding word attached. Check the rendered
	text, not the source, after editing this prose.

	bands={false}: the margin animation holds ~47 MB of decoded frames on every
	page, and this one needs its memory budget for two windows of its own.
*/}
<PageLayout bands={false} meta={meta}>
	<PageTitle
		title="Watching a universe grow"
		subtitle="Two universes from identical starting conditions. Change the recipe and watch where they part company."
	/>

	<p class="explore-status" role="status" data-status hidden></p>

	<div class="demo-structure" data-demo data-pagefind-body>
		<div class="demo-stage" data-stage>
			{/*
				The A/B switch only exists below 40em, where two panels would be 173px
				wide and ~600px apart -- never on screen together, which defeats the
				comparison. It is a radiogroup, so arrow keys flick between the two
				universes and give a blink comparator for free.
			*/}
			<div class="demo-panel-switch" data-panel-switch hidden>
				<span class="demo-chips__label" id="sg-panel-label">Showing</span>
				<div class="demo-chips__set" data-chips="panel" aria-labelledby="sg-panel-label"></div>
				<p class="demo-hint">Flick between them — same patch of sky, so the differences jump out.</p>
			</div>

			<div class="demo-stage__pair" data-pair>
				<section class="demo-panel" aria-labelledby="sg-a-name" data-panel="a">
					<h3 class="demo-panel__name" id="sg-a-name"><i data-dot="a"></i>Universe A</h3>
					<div class="demo-panel__frame" data-frame>
						<canvas data-canvas-a role="img"></canvas>
						<div class="demo-panel__placeholder" data-placeholder hidden></div>
					</div>
					<div class="demo-panel__chips">
						<div class="demo-chips__row">
							<span class="demo-chips__label" id="sg-om-label">Matter</span>
							<div class="demo-chips__set" data-chips="a-om" aria-labelledby="sg-a-name sg-om-label"></div>
						</div>
						<div class="demo-chips__row">
							<span class="demo-chips__label" id="sg-w0-label">Dark energy</span>
							<div class="demo-chips__set" data-chips="a-w0" aria-labelledby="sg-a-name sg-w0-label"></div>
						</div>
					</div>
				</section>

				<section class="demo-panel" aria-labelledby="sg-b-name" data-panel="b">
					<h3 class="demo-panel__name" id="sg-b-name"><i data-dot="b"></i>Universe B</h3>
					<div class="demo-panel__frame" data-frame>
						<canvas data-canvas-b role="img"></canvas>
						<div class="demo-panel__placeholder" data-placeholder hidden></div>
					</div>
					<div class="demo-panel__chips">
						<div class="demo-chips__row">
							<span class="demo-chips__label">Matter</span>
							<div class="demo-chips__set" data-chips="b-om" aria-labelledby="sg-b-name sg-om-label"></div>
						</div>
						<div class="demo-chips__row">
							<span class="demo-chips__label">Dark energy</span>
							<div class="demo-chips__set" data-chips="b-w0" aria-labelledby="sg-b-name sg-w0-label"></div>
						</div>
					</div>
				</section>
			</div>

			{/*
				Sticky to the BOTTOM of the stage, not under the page title: it is a
				video transport and belongs under the picture, and the quantitative
				chart below is driven by the same clock, so it must stay reachable
				while the reader looks at it.
			*/}
			<div class="demo-transport">
				<div class="demo-transport__buttons">
					<button type="button" data-reset aria-label="Back to the beginning">&#9198;</button>
					<button type="button" data-step-back aria-label="One step earlier">&#9664;</button>
					<button type="button" class="demo-transport__play" data-play aria-label="Play the animation"
						>Play</button
					>
					<button type="button" data-step-fwd aria-label="One step later">&#9654;</button>
				</div>
				<div class="demo-zslider">
					<input type="range" data-time min="0" max="38" step="1" aria-label="Cosmic time" />
					<p class="demo-zslider__scale" aria-hidden="true">
						<span>z = 45</span>
						<span data-z-readout>z = 45.11</span>
						<span>now</span>
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<p class="demo-hint" data-motion-hint hidden>Step or drag to run time forward.</p>
		</div>

		<div class="demo-shared">
			<div class="demo-chips__row">
				<span class="demo-chips__label" id="sg-norm-label">Matched</span>
				<div class="demo-chips__set" data-chips="norm" aria-labelledby="sg-norm-label"></div>
			</div>
			<div class="demo-gridmap" data-gridmap aria-hidden="true"></div>
			<p class="demo-progress" data-progress>
				The grid is still running: <span data-fill="nAvail">2</span> of <span data-fill="nTotal">17</span> universes are finished. Last exported <span data-fill="exported"></span>.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="demo-chips__row">
			<span class="demo-chips__label" id="sg-view-label">Chart</span>
			<div class="demo-chips__set" data-chips="view" aria-labelledby="sg-view-label"></div>
		</div>

		<div class="demo-chart demo-chart--structure">
			<canvas data-chart role="img"
				aria-label="A chart comparing the two universes: growth rate, clumpiness, or clustering scale by scale."
			></canvas>
		</div>

		<p class="visually-hidden" data-live aria-live="polite"></p>
		<p class="demo-caption" data-caption></p>

		<div class="demo-why">
			<h2>Why this matters</h2>
			<p>
				Both universes here begin from <em>the same</em> random pattern of tiny
				ripples — the same seed, the same box, the same everything except the recipe.
				At redshift 45 they are almost indistinguishable: the pattern is identical
				because the seed is, and only the contrast differs, by around a fifth.
				Everything that separates them afterwards is gravity working against
				expansion, and the only thing that differs is how much matter there is to
				pull and how the dark energy behaves.
			</p>
			<p>
				That is the whole method of modern cosmology in one image. We cannot rerun
				the Universe with a different amount of matter, so we run the simulation
				instead, and compare the web it grows against the one we see in galaxy
				surveys. The growth rate chart is where the difference is starkest: across the
				matter axis it nearly doubles. Redshift-space distortions in galaxy surveys
				constrain the combination <i>f</i>&sigma;<sub>8</sub> rather than <i>f</i>
				on its own, because <i>f</i> alone cannot be separated from how galaxies
				trace the matter.
			</p>
			<p>
				The <strong>Matched</strong> control is the subtle one. Simulations are
				normally tuned so every universe has the same clumpiness <em>today</em>,
				which makes them agree now and differ only in how they got here. Match them
				at the <em>start</em> instead and they begin together and end apart. Neither
				is more correct — they answer different questions, and switching between
				them on the clumpiness chart is the quickest way to see why that matters.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="demo-deep">
			<h2>The detail</h2>

			<p>
				Dark-matter-only runs with <a href="https://swift.strw.leidenuniv.nl/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">SWIFT</a>,
				from initial conditions generated with MUSIC using an Eisenstein&ndash;Hu
				transfer function and second-order Lagrangian perturbation theory. Each box
				is <span data-fill="box">100</span>&thinsp;Mpc/<i>h</i> on a side and
				holds <span data-fill="particles">2,097,152</span> particles
				(128<sup>3</sup>), integrated from <i>z</i>&thinsp;=&thinsp;50 to 0
				with <span data-fill="frames">39</span> snapshots kept. The slider starts at
				<i>z</i>&thinsp;=&thinsp;45 because SWIFT does not write an output for the
				scale factor it begins on.
			</p>

			<p>
				Every run in the grid shares one random seed
				(<span data-fill="seed">20260825</span>), which is what makes the panels
				comparable at all. &Omega;<sub>b</sub> scales with &Omega;<sub>m</sub> at a
				fixed baryon fraction of 0.1517. That keeps the baryon feature in the
				transfer function from doing work that belongs to the matter density —
				holding &Omega;<sub>b</sub> fixed instead would push the baryon fraction to
				0.30 at the sparse end. Only the fiducial point has a baryon density
				consistent with the measured one; the others are deliberate variations, not
				candidate universes. The dark-energy axis varies <i>w</i><sub>0</sub>; because
				&rho;<sub>DE</sub>&thinsp;&prop;&thinsp;<i>a</i><sup>&minus;3(1+<i>w</i>)</sup>, <i>w</i><sub>0</sub>&thinsp;=&thinsp;&minus;1.3 means dark
				energy <em>grows</em> with time rather than weakening — the intuitive reading of
				the sign is backwards, which is why the control says so in words. Each grid
				point appears here as its run finishes; the growth curves are analytic and
				are drawn for every cosmology whether or not its images exist yet.
			</p>

			<p>
				Each frame is a <span data-fill="slab">15</span>&thinsp;Mpc/<i>h</i> slab
				projected to <span data-fill="res">512</span><sup>2</sup> pixels with an SPH
				kernel over 64 neighbours. Pixel value is
				log<sub>10</sub>(1&thinsp;+&thinsp;&delta;) mapped onto the same black and
				white points, <span data-fill="stretchLo">-0.9</span> to <span data-fill="stretchHi">1.8</span>,
				for every frame and every run — a
				shared stretch is what makes the growth real rather than an artefact of
				renormalising each picture.
			</p>

			<p>
				On the clustering chart, each curve stops where the measurement does. A
				100&thinsp;Mpc/<i>h</i> box with two million particles has a white shot-noise
				floor of about 1.37&thinsp;Mpc<sup>3</sup>, and at early times the real
				clustering is beneath it — at redshift 45 only a handful of the 35 scales
				rise above the noise. Those points are not plotted rather than being drawn as
				noise. Nothing is plotted below the box's own fundamental
				mode, <i>k</i>&thinsp;=&thinsp;0.044&thinsp;Mpc<sup>&minus;1</sup>, which is a
				limit of the simulation rather than of the Universe — a 100&thinsp;Mpc/<i>h</i>
				box does not contain the baryon acoustic scale at all. It also holds only
				about 29 halos above 10<sup>14</sup>&thinsp;M<sub>&#9737;</sub>/<i>h</i>, so
				nothing here should be read as a claim about how many clusters exist.
			</p>

			<div class="demo-downloads">
				<a href="/data/structure-growth/README.md">Data notes</a>
				<a href="/data/structure-growth/structure.json">structure.json</a>
				<a href="/data/structure-growth/pk.json">pk.json</a>
				<a
					href="https://github.com/SKoonkor/skoonkor.github.io/blob/main/tools/structure/export_web.py"
					rel="noopener"
					target="_blank">build script</a
				>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>

	<noscript>
		<p>
			This demo animates simulation frames in your browser, so it needs JavaScript.
			The underlying numbers are readable without it: see the
			<a class="cactus-link" href="/data/structure-growth/README.md">data notes</a>.
		</p>
	</noscript>
</PageLayout>

<script>
	import { init } from "@/scripts/explore/structure-growth.js";

	const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-demo]");
	if (root) void init(root);
</script>
